================
STP Attack Prevention

Why we did this
================
Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) protects the network from Layer 2 loops.

However, attackers can exploit STP by sending malicious BPDUs and manipulating the network topology.

Common STP attacks include:

- Rogue Root Bridge Attack
- BPDU Spoofing
- STP Manipulation
- Unexpected Topology Changes
- Layer 2 Loop Creation

Impact:

- Network instability
- Traffic redirection
- Broadcast storms
- Man-in-the-Middle opportunities
- Network outages

To secure STP, Cisco recommends multiple protection mechanisms working together.

This approach is called STP Attack Prevention.

What will happen
================
1. Access ports use PortFast.
2. BPDU Guard protects edge ports.
3. Root Guard protects Root Bridge election.
4. Loop Guard prevents loop creation.
5. Trunk links are secured.
6. Rogue switches cannot influence STP topology.
7. Network remains stable and predictable.

